import Clip from '../../components/Clip.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro';
import FortisPerceptionDiagram from '../../components/FortisPerceptionDiagram.astro';
import {
  capabilityTiers,
  chassisRequirements,
  skidSteer,
  actuators,
  armResults,
  orbitResults,
  orbitConditions,
  architecture,
} from '../../data/fortis';

const entry = await getEntry('projects', 'fortis');
if (!entry) throw new Error('Missing project entry: fortis');
---

<CaseStudy
  entry={entry}
  standfirst="A tethered inspection robot for the inside of a fusion reactor. I owned the software stack, the simulation program and the perception and state estimation for it, and this page is organized around the problems that had to be solved to make it move, see and stay upright."
>
  <h2>The problem</h2>

  <p>
    DIII-D is a fusion research tokamak at General Atomics. Inspecting the
    vessel interior is manual work in a confined, delicate space, and the
    sponsor wanted a tethered robot that could enter, orbit toroidally along
    the floor, and carry an arm for camera inspection and thermocouple
    placement on plasma-facing components.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-tokamak-context" eager />

  <p>
    The vessel sets the rules. The only way in is the R0 port and a narrow
    entrance tunnel. Inside is a graphite-walled interior with a low-friction
    floor and a 4.5 inch step between the diverter and floor planes. Nothing may
    be modified or contacted, which rules out rails, fixtures and magnets. Every
    external dimension of the robot was fixed before the team drew anything, and
    there was no budget for physical prototypes, so every sizing decision had to
    be made in simulation and be right the first time.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...chassisRequirements} />

  <DataTable {...capabilityTiers} />

  <FigurePair ids={['fortis-r0-port', 'fortis-diverter-step']} />

  <h2>What I owned</h2>

  <p>
    I was the technical lead on a five-person capstone team. The entire software
    stack, all of the simulation work, the perception and state estimation
    stack, the drivetrain and arm sizing studies, the system design of the
    chassis and arm, the containerized development environment and the Jetson
    Orin bring-up were mine, along with the technical direction and the sprint
    reviews that gated design decisions. Teammates carried fabrication and
    assembly, the power distribution system and a share of the CAD drafting
    under my direction. The hardware demonstrations at the bottom were run by
    the whole team.
  </p>

  <h2>Problem: the physics engine cannot model an omni wheel</h2>

  <p>
    Everything downstream depended on simulating a holonomic drive on a
    low-friction floor, and Isaac Sim has no anisotropic friction. An omni
    wheel grips along its drive direction and rolls freely across it on passive
    rollers, and there is no way to declare a contact material that behaves
    differently along two axes. A low-friction cylinder has no traction to
    drive on, a high-friction cylinder cannot strafe, and anything in between
    does both badly.
  </p>

  <p>
    I turned the material property into geometry: each perimeter roller became
    a chain of five spheres, and the whole wheel a hub carrying rings of those
    chains. Sphere contacts grip in every direction individually, but the chain
    is free to spin about the roller axis, so the assembly as a whole rolls
    freely across the drive direction. That is the anisotropy the material
    system could not express.
  </p>

  <p>
    The substitution has a known bias, and characterizing it mattered more than
    hiding it. Sphere chains generate more resistance than real free-spinning
    rollers, roughly 20 percent more torque on matched runs, so every motor
    margin the simulation reported is conservative by construction. A model
    biased the other way would have been useless for sizing motors.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-isaac-viewport" />

  <h2>Problem: the team's skid-steer chassis could not cross the step</h2>

  <p>
    The team started on skid-steer because it is the cheapest thing to build.
    To orbit the vessel from its landing orientation, a skid-steer robot has to
    perform a 90 degree point turn while straddling the step. I simulated that
    turn across 60 geometrically valid configurations, sweeping wheel radius,
    wheel width, wheelbase, track width and motor torque.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...skidSteer} />

  <p>
    Three configurations completed the turn, and all three reached 85 to 116
    degrees of tilt and drifted 7 to 10 inches radially. Next to plasma-facing
    components that is a failure regardless of whether the yaw target was met.
    The cause is geometric, not a tuning problem: during a point turn every
    wheel arc crosses the step circle, so the wheels must climb a wall nearly
    equal to their own radius, and no wheelbase or track width in the buildable
    range avoids it. The study also used a friction coefficient more generous
    than the measured graphite floor.
  </p>

  <p>
    That study redirected the architecture to an omnidirectional X-drive on
    omni wheels in a chamfered square chassis. An X-drive strafes directly
    along the toroidal orbit with no yaw required to translate, so the wheels
    never cross the step at all; the robot straddles it for the whole orbit.
    X-drive beat mecanum on mechanical grounds: 90 degree rollers are simpler
    than 45 degree ones, 8 inch omni wheels were available off the shelf, and
    45 degree corner mounting falls naturally out of the chamfered chassis.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-endoskeleton-cad" />

  <h2>Problem: sizing the drive motors for a floor nobody could test on</h2>

  <p>
    With the wheel model in place I ran one full orbit at each commanded speed
    from 0.10 to 0.30 m/s, at the mission radius, straddling the step, with the
    arm stowed, and logged per-wheel torque.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...orbitConditions} />

  <DataTable {...orbitResults} />

  <p>
    Worst-wheel 95th-percentile torque reaches 4.10 Nm at 0.30 m/s against 4.98
    Nm of continuous motor capability at 60 A, so the drive stays inside
    continuous rating across the whole speed range with the conservative wheel
    model. Peak excursions of 10 to 11 Nm are short roller-contact spikes well
    inside transient capability.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-orbit-torque" />

  <p>
    The front-left wheel trends higher than the other three throughout the
    sweep because the arm center of gravity sits forward of chassis center. The
    orbit runs in a fixed direction, so the load biases onto the front pair and
    stays there. That asymmetry is why the margin is quoted against the worst
    wheel rather than the mean.
  </p>

  <Clip id="orbitSim" />

  <h2>Problem: sizing arm actuators over a joint space that is mostly invalid</h2>

  <p>
    The arm has to reach 30 inches across the sponsor's approved envelope and
    fold small enough to enter through the same port as the chassis. Sampling
    its joint space blindly wastes almost every sample, because most of joint
    space puts the arm through the chassis, through the floor or outside the
    vessel, and any torque statistic computed over those poses describes a
    robot that cannot exist.
  </p>

  <p>
    I wrote a constraint-aware Monte Carlo: 5,000 poses drawn uniformly across
    the four joints, with the 3 lb payload applied in every sample to hold the
    worst case, then filtered for chassis, self and floor collision and for the
    reactor envelope. Before trusting any of it I verified the sampler was
    uniform across each joint range, because pass rates over a biased draw are
    not pass rates.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...armResults} />

  <Figure id="fortis-mc-sampling" />

  <p>
    Each surviving pose was checked for stability by projecting the total
    center of gravity against the chassis support polygon, with the physics run
    at the lightest plausible chassis mass in the design range rather than the
    expected one, since a lighter chassis tips more easily under arm
    cantilever. Any pose that survives at the light mass is safe at the real
    one. The filtered set then became the shared input for both the joint
    torque analysis and the chassis stability analysis, so actuator sizing and
    tip-over margin were computed against the same population of poses instead
    of two different robots.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-poloidal-map" />

  <DataTable {...actuators} />

  <p>
    Every joint peaks below 60 percent of its continuous rating. The shoulder is
    the loaded joint at 17.62 Nm peak against a 30 Nm rating, roughly 1.7 times
    margin at the single worst pose in the reachable set. No pose in the set is
    analytically unstable; 25 tipped in physics under the deliberately light
    chassis mass, all inside the support polygon analytically, which is dynamic
    rocking on the step rather than a static failure.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-arm-torque" />

  <Clip id="armSim" />

  <p>
    The same loop settled the arm geometry. A 50 inch arm covered more vessel
    per chassis position but needed actuators that broke the mass and cost
    budget and raised the tipping problem for the chassis carrying them. At 30
    inches the demand stayed inside the torque range of affordable steppers.
    Moving the links from aluminum to carbon fiber tube cut link mass and let
    the wiring route internally, and three nesting links fold to a 16.877 by
    2.744 inch stow profile, which is what lets a 30 inch arm enter through an
    opening shorter than itself.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-arm-stow" />

  <h2>Problem: motion must never happen outside a permitted mission phase</h2>

  <p>
    Inside a vessel that must not be touched, an arm or drive command arriving
    at the wrong moment is the worst failure available. I built the stack so
    that motion is state-gated. A mission state machine holds nine states and
    fifteen events, with transitions and their guards expressed as a data table
    rather than nested conditionals. It is pure logic with no middleware or
    hardware dependencies, which is what let the transition table be exercised
    exhaustively before any of it ran on a robot.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-software-layers" />

  <p>
    Every action component sits above the state machine rather than beside it.
    The drive component rejects any motion command issued outside a permitted
    phase and emits an explicit stop on rejection rather than silently dropping
    the command, so a rejected command is observable. The arm component was
    scaffolded against the same contract before its kinematics existed, which is
    why inverse kinematics and the motion action dropped in later without
    renegotiating the interface.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two properties matter more than the state count. Any state can transition to
    the fault state through an unguarded wildcard, which is what lets the drive
    health monitor latch a fault from anywhere in the mission. And leaving the
    fault state requires an explicit operator acknowledgement, so recovery is
    never automatic: a drive that comes back healthy on its own does not clear
    the fault, because the condition that caused it has not been inspected.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-mission-states" />

  <h2>Problem: the simulation model and the robot code drifting apart</h2>

  <p>
    Operator teleoperation publishes a body velocity command. An X-drive
    kinematics component maps it to four wheel velocity setpoints, and those go
    out over a daisy-chained CAN bus to four controllers running onboard
    velocity control. The kinematics geometry constants are pinned to the robot
    description by a regression test that fails the moment either side moves,
    so the model the simulation sized against and the code the robot runs
    cannot diverge quietly.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-block-diagram" />

  <p>
    Coordinated multi-axis stepping for the arm runs on a dedicated
    microcontroller rather than the Jetson, because a step pulse delayed by the
    operating system scheduler is a lost position on a geared stepper, not a
    late one. The host owns the analytic inverse kinematics and the motion
    action interface, the microcontroller owns step timing, and the two meet at
    a serial contract with heartbeat and status reporting.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-arm-subsystem" />

  <h2>Problem: five engineers, five machines, one term</h2>

  <p>
    The stack is a containerized ROS 2 Humble environment bundling the
    middleware, motion planning, the controller framework and a visualization
    bridge, so every team member ran a byte-identical stack regardless of
    machine. Environment drift stopped being a category of problem. Continuous
    integration builds and tests the workspace on every change, and linting
    runs both on commit and in the pipeline.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...architecture} />

  <h2>Problem: noticing what changed inside the vessel between runs</h2>

  <p>
    Five depth cameras feed the stack: four on the chassis faces through a
    powered USB 3 hub with a single uplink to the Jetson, and one on the arm.
    Each camera back-projects its aligned depth into a point cloud in its own
    optical frame, the clouds are transformed into the body frame and merged on
    a fixed-rate timer, and the merged cloud integrates into a persistent voxel
    map. A map saved on one run becomes the reference for the next, and the
    difference is published both as visual markers and as a machine-readable
    summary. For an inspection robot, that diff is the product rather than a
    debugging aid. Object detection runs YOLOv8 through ONNX inference on the
    same streams.
  </p>

  <p>
    Visual odometry runs off the front camera and is keyframe-anchored: every
    frame is matched against the current keyframe rather than the previous
    frame, so per-frame matching noise does not compound between keyframe
    refreshes and drift accrues per keyframe instead of per frame. Motion is
    recovered by lifting matched features through depth and solving for pose
    with outlier rejection.
  </p>

  <FortisPerceptionDiagram />

  <p>
    The depth cameras cannot stream inside the development container, so I
    built a synthetic camera rig that publishes the exact topic contract of the
    real hardware, including aligned depth and inertial data for one to four
    mounts. The whole perception chain is developed and tested against it and
    then verified live on the Jetson, which is what makes the perception tests
    runnable by anyone on the team without the robot.
  </p>

  <Clip id="visionUi" />

  <h2>Problem: wheel slip poisons the heading estimate</h2>

  <p>
    An Extended Kalman Filter on the Jetson fuses wheel odometry, visual
    odometry and inertial data into one continuous pose estimate. The
    engineering is in what is deliberately excluded. Wheel odometry contributes
    translation only, never heading, because the rear wheels slip under load
    and a slipping wheel injects a phantom yaw rate the gyroscope never sees,
    which makes wheel-derived heading actively misleading rather than merely
    noisy. Visual odometry also contributes translation only, because its
    rotation quality collapses as feature count drops while the gyroscope stays
    uniform. The gyroscope owns yaw outright.
  </p>

  <p>
    Neither translation source is fused as an absolute position either, only as
    a velocity, because both are open-loop dead reckoning with no absolute
    reference, and feeding their absolute position in would inject unbounded
    drift as though it were a measurement. The two fail in different ways, one
    by slip and one by tracking loss, so each is followed according to its own
    stamped covariance and neither is ever trusted for heading.
  </p>

  <h2>What ran on hardware</h2>

  <p>
    Holonomic chassis drive under operator command with closed-loop wheel
    velocity, and toroidal orbit motion proved on hardware. Visual odometry
    running across four simultaneous camera streams with inertial data
    retrieval. Commanded arm motion through the reach envelope with object pick
    and stow, and the payload retained through the stow motion.
  </p>

  <Clip id="toroidal" />
  <Clip id="holonomic" />
  <Clip id="portEntry" />
  <Clip id="pickStow" />
  <Clip id="payloadHold" />

  <h2>The build</h2>

  <p>
    The design loop that produced all of the above: CAD geometry with real
    masses and materials went into the simulation, out came torque, speed and
    stability sweeps, and a sprint review gated on whether the criteria were
    met. Geometry did not advance until numbers existed for it.
  </p>

